<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:task-classification -->
## Task Classification

Before acting on a request, or before spawning any subagent, classify it into
one class and act (or delegate) accordingly. Do not default everything to
your most capable model or skip classification because a task "looks small."

| Class | Model tier | Effort | Examples |
|---|---|---|---|
| mechanical | cheapest/fastest | none/low | git ops, renames, formatting, lookups, version bumps |
| implementation | mid-tier | medium | writing/editing code, standard feature work, tests |
| debugging | mid-tier | high | diagnosing failures, races, regressions, bisecting |
| architecture | top-tier | high | redesigns, tradeoff analysis, multi-file reasoning |
| extreme | top-tier, long-horizon | high | codebase-wide rewrites, multi-system migrations, RFCs |

Rules:
- When spawning a subagent, pass the model tier matching the task class —
  never default all subagents to the most expensive model.
- If a task doesn't clearly match one class, don't force it — abstain and
  leave the current model/effort unchanged rather than guessing a tier.
- Re-classify mid-task if scope changes (a "mechanical" rename that turns
  into an API redesign is now "architecture").
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:task-classification -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:search-relevance -->
## Search & Relevance Protocol

Relevance is judged by the model reading results in-context, not by a
separate classifier — so the quality of what you retrieve directly gates the
quality of what you can judge. Apply this before trusting search output:

- **Narrow before broad.** Prefer a scoped Grep/Glob (specific pattern, path,
  file type) over a wide fishing query — every false positive returned is a
  token the model must personally triage.
- **Classify raw hits before acting on them.** A grep/glob match is a
  syntactic hit, not a semantic one — confirm relevance by reading enough
  context around the match before treating it as an answer.
- **Match the tool to the query shape.** Known pattern/symbol → direct
  Grep/Glob. Fuzzy or exploratory ("how does X work," "where is Y handled")
  → a read-only search subagent that returns a distilled answer, not raw
  matches, into your context.
- **Delegate wide-net searches.** If a search would return more than a
  screenful of undifferentiated matches, delegate the search-and-filter step
  to a subagent instead of paging through raw output inline — this keeps the
  relevance judgment out of your own context budget.
- **Prefer structural filters over content filters when available.** A path
  or file-type constraint you already know is true costs nothing and cuts
  the candidate set before any content matching runs.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:search-relevance -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:process-hygiene -->
## Context & Process Hygiene

- **Clear stale sessions.** When resuming work after a long gap (new
  calendar day, or wall-clock time much greater than active work time),
  checkpoint progress into a short handoff note and start fresh rather than
  continuing in an old session's accumulated context — this holds even for a
  direct continuation of the same task.
- **Delegate long sequential traces.** If a single turn is running long
  (dozens of sequential exploration/verification calls against the same
  project) — including checking status/log/diff or reading files across
  multiple git worktrees of the same repo — delegate the remaining work to a
  subagent instead of continuing inline.
- **Avoid redundant verification calls.** Before re-fetching state you
  already have (re-navigating a tab, re-reading a file you just wrote,
  re-running a check you just ran), check whether you already know the
  answer from earlier in the session. This applies to any file read
  earlier in the same session, not only ones you wrote or edited — before
  re-reading a file already in this session's context, check whether it
  changed since (by you or externally); if not, reuse what you already
  have instead of re-fetching it.
- **Avoid blind bulk edits on structured files.** Use targeted, verified
  edits on config/source files instead of regex/sed sweeps — a broken bulk
  edit triggers an expensive diagnose-fix-rebuild loop.
- **Audit periodically, don't guess.** If trace/observability data is
  available, periodically audit real sessions for waste (duplicate calls,
  retry loops, outlier-cost sessions) and turn confirmed patterns into rules
  here rather than optimizing on intuition.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:process-hygiene -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:verification-reporting -->
## Verification Reporting

Never run tests, typecheck, lint, build, ad-hoc verification scripts (e.g.
Playwright/Node one-offs), or process management (starting, stopping, or
killing dev servers) directly in the main agent's own shell — always delegate
the run to an independent subagent (via the Agent tool) and read its full raw
output before reporting a result, even for a single quick check. This applies
to every project and every skill, not just one. A subagent's pass/fail
summary is not evidence on its own — always read the raw command output it
returns before claiming success. Size what you ask the subagent to report to
the outcome:
- On any failure or ambiguous result, require the complete raw output
  (stdout/stderr, exit code) — you cannot diagnose from a summary.
- On a clean pass, ask only for the command(s) run, the exit code, a
  one-line result, and one representative raw-output sample per distinct
  scenario — never a dump of every repeated call in a passing loop or
  threshold test.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:verification-reporting -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:scope-discipline -->
## Scope Discipline

Do exactly what was asked, no more.

- Do not add selectors, tests, refactors, or documentation sections that were not requested — including things that seem obviously useful.
- Never delete an existing exported symbol, selector, or function unless the user explicitly asked, or you have proven it is dead code and stated the evidence for that claim.
- Do not create commits that were not requested. If uncommitted changes exist and it's unclear whether to commit, stage them and ask rather than committing.
- If you notice something else worth changing while working, note it separately (a `proposed-additions` list, a follow-up suggestion) instead of doing it inline.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:scope-discipline -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:repo-preflight -->
## Repo & Branch Pre-Flight

Before running an install/apply-template step, or before rebasing or picking a base branch for a PR or PR stack:

- State the resolved target directory and confirm via `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` which repo you're actually operating in — do not assume the current working directory matches the intended target.
- Re-fetch remote refs (`git fetch origin`) before computing divergence, merge-base, or a rebase/PR base branch. Never trust a stale local ref for this.
- When multiple worktrees or stacked branches exist, confirm the active worktree before editing.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:repo-preflight -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:ai-generated-text -->
## AI-Generated Text Conventions

All AI-generated text — code comments, docstrings, PR/commit descriptions,
generated docs — is written for a real person reading the current codebase,
not for a changelog or a future auditor of what changed.

- **Audience is a person, not a diff.** Write for someone who wants to
  understand what the code does and why, right now — never "used by X",
  "added for the Y fix", or "handles the case from issue #123."
- **Never narrate history.** Describe the current state only. No "previously
  this did X, now it does Y," no changelog-style entries embedded in code,
  docs, or comments, and no explaining the sequence of changes that produced
  the current state.
- **One paragraph per concept.** A single explanation (docstring, comment
  block, section of generated docs) covers one concept in one paragraph. If
  a second paragraph feels needed, it's a second concept — split it or cut
  it.
- **Comments have a length ceiling.** A variable/method/function comment is
  one line. If the reasoning needs more than a line, question whether the
  code itself should be clearer instead of leaning on prose.
- **Verbosity is capped throughout.** Default to the shortest text that
  fully conveys the point — this applies to comments, generated docs, and
  agent-authored prose alike, not just user-facing chat responses.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:ai-generated-text -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:response-formatting -->
## Response Formatting

Use markdown structure in chat responses whenever it improves scannability —
this is about how answers are presented, not how much is said.

- **Highlight key terms and identifiers.** Bold the load-bearing nouns (file
  names, flags, decisions) so a skimming reader catches them.
- **Use tables for comparisons or multi-attribute lists.** Options,
  tradeoffs, before/after states — anything with 2+ rows and 2+ columns of
  structured data.
- **Use headers only for multi-section answers.** A single short answer
  doesn't need a header; a response covering several distinct points does.
- **Prefer structure over prose density**, but do not pad — a one-line
  answer stays one line. This section governs formatting choices, not
  response length; length/verbosity limits (e.g. an active Concise output
  style) still take precedence.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:response-formatting -->
